build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
# A built wheel, anywhere. `dist/` already covers the ordinary case — `python -m build` writes
# there — but a `pip wheel .` or a `--outdir .` puts one at the repository ROOT, outside every
# rule above, and one sat there untracked across five rounds: `synapse_cdm-1.0.0-py3-none-any.whl`,
# one `git add -A` from being committed. Committing a wheel would break
# `tests/test_cdm_packaging.py` and `gates/wheel_install.py`, which compare a BUILT wheel's
# manifest against `git ls-files` in both directions — a tracked wheel makes the artefact part of
# its own input. The rule is the extension and not the one filename, for the reason the archive
# block below gives about itself: a `.whl` is never an artefact this repository tracks, so the rule
# costs nothing and the next stray one does not get to be the test case.
*.whl
.DS_Store

# Documentation site (Docusaurus)
docs/node_modules/
docs/build/
docs/.docusaurus/
docs/.cache-loader/
.wrangler/

# Pinned specification documents. NEVER COMMITTED — and until this block existed that was true
# by discipline plus a test, which is a gate on the COMMIT and not on the operation.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS CLOSES. `tests/test_cdm_pins.py::test_no_pdf_is_tracked_anywhere_in_the_
# repository` asserts `git ls-files` returns no `.pdf` anywhere. It is a good gate and it fires
# after the fact: `git add -A` from the repository root stages every held document, and only a
# suite run or a reading of `git status` catches it before the commit. That near-miss has happened
# twice. The rule below makes the staging itself refuse, so the gate and the mechanism now fail in
# the same direction and neither is load-bearing alone.
#
# THE EXTENSION SET IS DERIVED, NOT GUESSED. Every file under a `fixtures/*/spec/` directory is a
# `.pdf` (60), a `.txt` (1), a `.json` (13: nine pin records, four `*_terms.json` provenance
# records) or a `.py` generator (9) — FOUR members since 2026-09-04; the note below the rules says
# why. The last two are the committed artefacts, so this ignores an EXTENSION, not the directory.
*.pdf
*.txt

# THE EXTENSION SET GAINED A FOURTH MEMBER ON 2026-09-04 AND THAT IS A FIRST. Every earlier
# correction to the derived-set paragraph above moved only the ARITHMETIC and said so, in the same
# words each time — "the load-bearing half, that the extension set is exactly those three and no
# other, was and is true, so the RULE never stopped working". **That half is what changed this
# time.** The text-pins round pinned a document whose publisher issues it as text, so the set is now
# four and the rule below is the consequence rather than a precaution. The three figures had ALSO
# drifted again, from 52 / 8 / 8 to 60 / 9 / 9, as the later lineages landed and nothing sent
# anybody back to a comment in this file — that is the fourth time, it is corrected in the same
# commit as the substantive change, and it is the less interesting of the two corrections.
#
# AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS KEPT TO FOUR LINES ON PURPOSE, which is the reason this note is here
# and not there. `*.pdf` is cited as `.gitignore:42` on twenty-eight lines of this tree (2026-09-16 count, derived by `tests/test_cdm_pins.py`); a fifth line above the rule
# would have re-pointed every one of them at a comment. The foot of this block states that rule for
# comments and this round found it applies to a REWRITE of an existing comment just as much.
#
# `*.txt` JOINS `*.pdf` ON THE SAME ARGUMENT AND NOT ON A LICENCE ONE, added 2026-09-04 by the
# text-pins round. IETF RFC 2781 is the first document this repository pins that its publisher
# issues as TEXT AND NOT AS A PDF — the RFC Editor's info page names exactly two formats, TXT and
# HTML, and there is no PDF route — so `fixtures/klv/spec/rfc2781.txt` is a pin, and without this
# rule it is the first held document since this block was written that `git add -A` would stage.
#
# STATED BECAUSE THE RULE READS BACKWARDS OTHERWISE: THIS IS NOT A LICENCE CLAIM. RFC 2781's own
# Full Copyright Statement permits copying it "without restriction of any kind" provided the notice
# travels with the copy, so committing it would have been ALLOWED — which is exactly what STANAG
# 4609 Edition 5 does not allow, and that restriction is the one `klv_pin.json`'s
# `not_committed.why` quotes. The ground here is ONE MECHANISM FOR EVERY PINNED DOCUMENT: a tree
# that vendored what it may and pinned what it may not would need two, and a reader could not tell
# from a pin record which one governed a given row. Ruled at `klv_pin.json`'s `text_pin_ruling`.
#
# GLOBAL RATHER THAN PATH-ANCHORED, DERIVED THE WAY THE `*.pdf` RULE ARGUES FOR ITSELF ABOVE:
# `git ls-files '*.txt'` is 0, so a global rule strands nothing in the index, and an extension rule
# needs no edit when the next `spec/` directory arrives. The three `.txt` populations already in the
# working tree keep the rules they already had — `*.egg-info/` at line 8, `docs/.gitignore` for
# `node_modules`, and the provenance directory rule at the foot of this file.
#
# AND ITS POSITION IS FORCED RATHER THAN PREFERRED, which is the one thing about this rule that is
# not obvious from reading it. Git resolves a path to its LAST matching pattern. A global `*.txt`
# placed BELOW `fixtures/klv/provenance/` would answer `git check-ignore -v` for the nine `.txt`
# CDX dumps under that directory with THIS line instead of that one — the same file reported
# against a different rule, and `tests/test_cdm_pins.py` asserts that directory's rule by line
# number. So this sits above it and loses to it on purpose.
#
# THE TWO CITED LINE NUMBERS BELOW MOVED BY FIFTY-TWO AND WERE RE-POINTED IN THIS COMMIT.
# `fixtures/klv/streams/` was `.gitignore:97` and is now `.gitignore:149`; `fixtures/klv/provenance/`
# was `.gitignore:121` and is now `.gitignore:173`. Both are cited BY LINE in `klv_pin.json`,
# `FORMAT_COVERAGE.md` and `tests/test_cdm_pins.py`, and the note at the foot of this block explains
# why a citation to a line is only worth having if the line holds still. Inserting here moved both,
# so every citation to either was updated in the same commit that moved it rather than left to a
# later sweep — the discipline this block asks of a derived COUNT, applied to a derived LINE NUMBER.
# `*.pdf` is untouched at line 42 and every citation to it still resolves.

#
# THOSE THREE COUNTS WERE 36 / 6 / 5 AND WERE STALE, corrected 2026-08-26 by the STANAG 4609
# round's sweep. They had drifted as the CAT023, CAT034, CAT048 and CAT062 lineages landed, because
# nothing sends anybody back to a comment in this file. The load-bearing half — that the extension
# set is exactly those three and no other — was and is true, so the RULE never stopped working;
# only the arithmetic beside it went wrong. Recorded rather than quietly fixed, because a number
# that describes itself as derived and is in fact typed is precisely the claim a disjunction sweep
# exists to catch. It is derived by:
#   find packages/cdm/synapse_cdm/fixtures -type f -path '*/spec/*' | sed 's/.*\.//' | sort | uniq -c
#
# THE GENERATOR COUNT MOVED FROM 5 TO 7 TO 8, and the third move is the first one that was not a
# repair. The KLV framing round of 2026-08-26 added `fixtures/klv/spec/build_fixtures.py`, so the
# count went stale the moment the file landed and was corrected in the same commit — which is the
# discipline the paragraph above asks for, applied by the round that created the drift instead of by
# a later sweep that found it. The extension SET did not change and the rule did not move.
#
# THE PDF COUNT MOVED FROM 47 TO 48 on 2026-08-26, by the KLV length round, which obtained MISB
# ST 0107.3 and pinned it at `fixtures/klv/spec/ST0107.3.pdf`. Corrected in the same commit that
# created the drift, which is what the paragraph above asks for and is the second time it has been
# done that way rather than left to a later sweep. The extension SET did not change and the rule did
# not move. Worth noting that this is the count the rule EXISTS for: the 48th document is a six-page
# free download that closed a park, so the number grows every time somebody does the cheap thing.
#
# THE PDF COUNT MOVED FROM 48 TO 52 on 2026-08-26, by the KLV park 13 adjudication round, which
# obtained four editions of the MISB 0601 series: EG 0601.1 as a PIN at
# `fixtures/klv/spec/EG0601.1.pdf` — the document that closed park 13 — and the initial release,
# ST 0601.4 and ST 0601.8 as the 0601 LINEAGE at `fixtures/klv/spec/history/`. Corrected in the same
# commit that created the drift, which is the third time it has been done that way rather than left
# to a later sweep. The extension SET did not change and the rule did not move.
#
# THIS ROUND ALSO ADDED THE FIFTH `spec/history/` DIRECTORY, and that is worth a line because the
# rule above is an EXTENSION rule and therefore did not need to notice. `fixtures/klv/spec/history/`
# joins cat023's, cat034's, cat048's and cat062's; `git check-ignore -v` was run on each of the four
# files that landed and each reports `.gitignore:42:*.pdf`, so the citation to that LINE still holds.
# A `spec/`-directory rule would have needed editing here; an extension rule did not, which is the
# argument the derived-set paragraph above makes about itself.
#
# THIS NOTE SITS BELOW THE RULE AND NOT ABOVE IT, deliberately. `*.pdf` is cited as `.gitignore:42`
# in `klv_pin.json` and in commit messages, and a citation to a LINE is only worth having if the
# line holds still — so a comment added above the rule would have silently re-pointed every one of
# those citations at a blank line. The correction goes underneath.


# Archives, because that is how a lineage arrives. `cat048_pin.json` records `bundle_url` as
# `https://www.eurocontrol.int/archive_download/all/node/11127` under the link text "Download all"
# — the 22-edition CAT048 lineage came down as one archive. Only `.zip` is evidenced by that
# record; the siblings are listed because they are the same class with the same failure mode and
# none of them is ever an artefact of this repository, so the rule costs nothing and the next
# publisher who serves a tarball does not get to be the test case.
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
fixtures/klv/streams/

# Fetched provenance evidence, because that is how a HELD file's origin gets established.
#
# `fixtures/klv/provenance/` holds what the Day Flight provenance round of 2026-08-26 fetched to
# answer one question about a file this repository already had: where did the pinned transport
# stream come from. The answer is byte identity against a served copy, and reaching it took Wayback
# CDX index dumps, archived pages of the MISB's own website, that site's machine-readable
# publication catalogues, and the sample collection's directory listings and index. All of it is
# EVIDENCE for sentences in FORMAT_COVERAGE.md and none of it is an artefact this repository ships.
#
# A DIRECTORY RULE AND NOT AN EXTENSION RULE, for the reason the `fixtures/klv/streams/` block
# above gives about itself and more so. The PDF rule works because a `spec/` directory holds three
# known extensions and a new document is the ordinary case. Fetched evidence has no extension
# discipline at all: this round alone landed `.txt` CDX dumps, `.html` archived pages, `.xml` site
# catalogues and one extensionless `00-README`, and the next round could just as well hold `.json`,
# `.warc` or a bare header dump. An extension rule would be a list somebody has to remember to
# extend, and the failure mode of forgetting is that somebody else's website enters the index.
#
# WHY NOT JUST LEAVE IT IN A TEMPORARY DIRECTORY. Because the standing rule is that every asserted
# fact comes from bytes HELD and PINNED, and a quotation from a page that was read and thrown away
# is a recollection. Each file here is pinned by SHA-256 and byte count in `klv_pin.json` under
# `day_flight_provenance_2026_08_26`, with the origin URL it was served from, exactly as the PDFs
# are. `git check-ignore -v` was run on every file that landed and each reports this rule.
fixtures/klv/provenance/

# Generated evidence records and the badges derived from them — round P4, 2026-09-08, M's
# pre-ruled default 1.
#
# WHY IT IS AT THE BOTTOM OF THIS FILE AND NOT BESIDE `*.whl`, WHICH IS WHERE IT BELONGS BY
# SUBJECT. Because `tests/test_cdm_pins.py` pins three of this file's lines BY NUMBER — 42
# (`*.pdf`), 43 (`*.txt`) and 173 (`fixtures/klv/provenance/`) — and `klv_pin.json` and
# `FORMAT_COVERAGE.md` cite them. A rule inserted above any of those silently re-points every
# citation at a different line, which is the defect that test exists to catch, and it caught this
# one: the first draft of this block went in above line 42 and reddened it. Appending is the only
# placement that leaves all three citations true, and git resolves a path to its LAST matching
# pattern, so an append is also the safest position for the rule itself.
#
# WHAT IT IGNORES. `synapse evidence generate --all` writes one JSON document per adapter under
# `evidence/<adapter>/<adapter-version>/evidence.json`, and `synapse badges` writes shields.io
# endpoint files under `evidence/badges/`. Each record NAMES THE COMMIT IT MEASURED and carries a
# sha256 of every fixture file the run read, so a tracked record is either stale the moment it is
# committed — it describes the tree as of its own generation, and the commit that adds it is a
# different tree — or a mechanical diff on every fixture round, in a file nobody reads and
# everybody re-generates.
#
# WHERE IT LIVES INSTEAD. CI generates the set on every run and uploads it as the artefact
# `evidence-<sha>`; the release round attaches it to the GitHub Release. That attachment is the
# moment `evidence.available` becomes true for a consumer who cannot run this package. It was
# false in every one of the fourteen manifests until the 2.1.2 Release carried the set — M ruled
# on 2026-09-08 that it stayed false through P4–P8, and it did — and since round PE (2026-09-12)
# every manifest declares it true and names that attachment. This paragraph went on saying "is
# false" until 2026-09-16, four days after PE flipped the manifests without coming back to it.
#
# ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND THE UNANCHORED FORM WAS A LIVE DEFECT RATHER THAN A STYLE
# POINT. A pattern with no leading slash matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, so a bare
# `evidence/` also ignored `schemas/evidence/` — the GENERATED AND TRACKED evidence schema this
# same round publishes. `git add` refused it and said which rule, which is the only reason this
# was found before the file went missing from a release. The generated records live at the
# repository root and nowhere else, so the rule says exactly that.
/evidence/

# THE DERIVED COUNTS ABOVE WERE CORRECTED AGAIN ON 2026-09-16, AND THIS TIME BY AN AUDIT RATHER
# THAN BY THE ROUND THAT MOVED THEM. `f28d58e` (2026-09-07) added four `*_terms.json` licensing
# provenance records under `spec/` — adsb, ais, legion, tak — and the derived-set paragraph went on
# saying `.json` pin record (9): the nine PIN records are still nine, but the paragraph enumerates
# every file under `spec/`, and the command it names returns 13. The citation count for
# `.gitignore:42` said twenty-four and never matched the tree that wrote it; the first correction
# said twenty-six, which was the count of the tree BEFORE this note existed — a note that names the
# citation is itself two more lines that carry it, and a figure derived before the sentence that
# states it is written is a figure derived over the wrong tree. `git grep -n '\.gitignore:42' | wc -l`
# gives 28 lines with this note in place, five of them in this file, and that is what the sentence
# now states; `tests/test_cdm_pins.py` re-derives the figure on every run, so the sixth drift fails
# the suite instead of waiting for a seventh audit.
# The `.pdf` (60) and `.txt` (1) figures are NOT re-derived here: this checkout holds no pinned
# document, so the command above cannot count them, and they stand as the text-pins round derived
# them. The evidence paragraph's "is false" was four days stale — round PE flipped every manifest
# on 2026-09-12 — and is corrected in place. Every line above 173 is where it was.
#
# Three rules follow, appended for the reason the evidence block gives: nothing above may move.

# The rounds apparatus and the agent configuration. Both are local, both are private, and both
# were kept out of the index by `.git/info/exclude` alone — a file that does not travel with the
# repository, so a second clone that copied the directories in had no rule and `git add -A` would
# have staged some two hundred files. That is the argument at the top of the pinned-document block,
# verbatim: a test on the commit is a gate on the COMMIT and not on the operation, and the
# mechanism has to be in the tree. The paths are not secret — forty tracked lines name `rounds/`
# (`git grep -n 'rounds/' | wc -l`, re-derived 2026-09-16 by `tests/test_cdm_pins.py`; the ruling
# read seventy, which was the tree before the same day's audit cut every pointer a clone could not
# follow) and the readiness report advertises `git ls-files rounds/` → 0 as a property of the tree
# — so ignoring them in the shared file gives away nothing and makes the advertised property one
# the tree itself enforces, at the lines that report now cites. Ruled by M on 2026-09-16.
rounds/
.claude/

# Ruff's cache, beside `.pytest_cache/` at line 3 for the same reason. It stayed out of `git status`
# only because ruff writes its own `.ruff_cache/.gitignore`, which exists once the tool has run
# here and not before, and a rule that depends on the tool having run is not a rule of this tree.
.ruff_cache/

# And two more, appended on 2026-09-16 below the three above for the same reason, by the audit
# that made the evidence records reproducible on another machine. The release gate writes
# `conformance-<version>.json` and `evidence-<version>.tar.gz` into the checkout root
# (`publish.yml`, the `gate` job) BEFORE the evidence step runs there, and `evidence.source_commit`
# reads `git status --porcelain` and appends `-dirty` to the commit when anything is unignored.
# So every record in `evidence-2.1.2.tar.gz` carries `source_commit: 5ab80f5…-dirty` for a file
# that is not a change to the tree, and `verify` masks the one field that binds a record to a
# commit for every consumer of that release. Anchored, like `/evidence/`, because the artefacts
# live at the root and nowhere else; the pipeline now also refuses a record whose commit is not
# HEAD, bare, so the next tag's records are bound rather than masked. The 2.1.2 records are not
# repairable — they can only be regenerated from the tag by a new release.
/conformance-*.json
/evidence-*.tar.gz
